diff --git a/specifications/docs/overview/Requests_and_responses.md b/specifications/docs/overview/Requests_and_responses.md
index bc37e680..373a8c16 100644
--- a/specifications/docs/overview/Requests_and_responses.md
+++ b/specifications/docs/overview/Requests_and_responses.md
@@ -239,33 +239,41 @@ To indicate the status of a request or operation, an appropriate HTTP status cod
 Status code `400` indicates normally a bad request, as well as a generic client-side error, used when no other `4xx` error code is appropriate.
 The client SHOULD NOT repeat the request without modifications.
 
-For `4xx` and `5xx` status codes, services MAY return additional error details if the `Prefer: return=representation` header is present in the request.
+## Error responses
+
+When a service returns a body with a `4xx` or `5xx` status code, the body MUST be a problem details object as defined in [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457). Its media type is `application/problem+json`, or `application/problem+xml` ([RFC 9457, Appendix B](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457#appendix-B)) when the request negotiated an XML representation. Services SHOULD return a problem details body with every `4xx` and `5xx` status code. When the `status` member is present, it MUST equal the HTTP status code of the response.
+
+The `type` member identifies the problem. Where one of the problem types below applies, services SHOULD use its type URI. Otherwise they use `about:blank`, which means the problem has no meaning beyond its HTTP status code ([RFC 9457, Section 4.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457#section-4.2.1)).
+
+| Type URI                                                                    | Status | Problem                                                                                                         |
+|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/validation-failed`     | 422    | The resource is well-formed, but it fails RM or template validation; `validationErrors` lists the failures       |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/unknown-ehr`           | 404    | No EHR with the given `ehr_id` exists                                                                           |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/unknown-template`      | 404    | No template with the given `template_id` exists                                                                 |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/ehr-already-exists`    | 409    | An EHR with the given `ehr_id`, or with the same subject id and namespace, already exists                        |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/template-already-exists` | 409  | A template with the same `template_id` already exists                                                           |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/query-version-exists`  | 409    | A stored query with the given `qualified_query_name` and `version` already exists                               |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/version-conflict`      | 412    | The `If-Match` request header does not name the latest version; the `ETag` header carries the latest `version_uid` |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/already-deleted`       | 400    | The resource identified by the request is already deleted                                                       |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/invalid-query`         | 400    | The query cannot be executed because of invalid input, such as malformed AQL or a missing or invalid parameter  |
+| `https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/query-timeout`         | 408    | The query reached the maximum execution time and the service aborted it                                         |
+
+Services MAY add extension members ([RFC 9457, Section 3.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457#section-3.2)). This specification defines one, `validationErrors`: a list with one entry per validation failure, each with a `message` and, where the failure concerns a node of the submitted resource, the RM `path` of that node.
+
+A problem details body SHOULD NOT carry clinical content or implementation internals such as stack traces ([RFC 9457, Section 5](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457#section-5)).
+
 Example error response:
 
 ```json
 {
-    "message": "Error message",
-    "code": 90000,
-    "errors": [
-        {
-            "_type": "DV_CODED_TEXT",
-            "value": "Error message",
-            "defining_code": {
-                "terminology_id": {
-                    "value": "local"
-                },
-                "code_string": "9000"
-            }
-        },
+    "type": "https://specifications.openehr.org/problems/its-rest/validation-failed",
+    "title": "Validation failed",
+    "status": 422,
+    "detail": "The COMPOSITION does not validate against template vital_signs.v1.",
+    "validationErrors": [
         {
-            "_type": "DV_CODED_TEXT",
-            "value": "Secondary error message",
-            "defining_code": {
-                "terminology_id": {
-                    "value": "local"
-                },
-                "code_string": "8000"
-            }
+            "message": "Missing mandatory node",
+            "path": "/content[openEHR-EHR-OBSERVATION.blood_pressure.v2]/data[at0001]"
         }
     ]
 }
